# MoOS ships a working secret store, because MoOS promises one.
#
# /etc/xdg-desktop-portal/kde-portals.conf routes org.freedesktop.impl.portal.Secret
# to kwallet. That promise is only kept if kwalletd is actually allowed to run:
# with [Wallet] Enabled=false, nothing owns org.freedesktop.secrets, the name is
# not even activatable on the session bus, and every application that stores a
# credential through libsecret fails at its first call.
#
# What that looked like in practice, on 2026-07-25: MoPlayer read its device id
# from the keyring during bootstrap, libsecret threw "Failed to unlock the
# keyring", the exception escaped main(), and the app died after its window
# existed but before its first frame — a BLACK WINDOW, with the real error
# visible only in the journal. Chromium, VS Code and anything else holding a
# token were failing the same way, more quietly.
#
# MoPlayer was fixed to survive a missing keyring (an app must never die for
# this), but the deeper defect was the OS shipping a Secret portal backed by a
# service it never guaranteed. This file is that guarantee.
#
# It is a DEFAULT, not a lock: /etc/xdg is the lowest layer of KDE's config
# stack, so ~/.config/kwalletrc still wins for anyone who deliberately turns the
# wallet off. moos-selfcheck reports when that is the case, so a disabled wallet
# stays a visible choice instead of an invisible breakage.
[Wallet]
Enabled=true
First Use=false
